      {
        "id": "def-normalizing-flow",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "A normalizing flow and its base density",
        "deps": [
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "def-measure-with-density",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable",
          "lem-c-one-diffeomorphisms-map-lebesgue-measurable-sets-to-lebesgue-measurable-sets"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $U,V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be nonempty open sets, and let $T:U\\to V$ be a bijection such that both $T$ and $T^{-1}$ are continuously differentiable. Here $C^1$ means totally differentiable at every point with continuous derivative matrix. Such a map is a **global $C^1$ diffeomorphism**. A finite composition $T=T_K\\circ\\cdots\\circ T_1$ of global $C^1$ diffeomorphisms between open subsets of $\\mathbb R^d$ is a **normalizing flow**.\n\nA **base density** on $U$ is a Lebesgue-measurable function $p_U:U\\to[0,\\infty]$ with $\\int_U p_U\\,d\\lambda_d=1$. It defines the probability measure $\\mu_U(A)=\\int_Ap_U\\,d\\lambda_d$ for Lebesgue-measurable $A\\subseteq U$.\n\nFor the model-measure construction, assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. The model measure on $V$ is $\\mu_X(B)=\\mu_U(T^{-1}(B))$ for Borel $B\\subseteq V$. This value is defined because the inverse $C^1$ diffeomorphism maps Borel sets to Lebesgue-measurable sets under Countable Choice. When this measure has a Lebesgue density, that density is the model density."
      },
